{0 Install}

This page sets up a project and makes a first component. The
{{!page-tutorial}tutorial} then adds a Remote call, a client file and a server.

{1:requirements Requirements}

- OCaml 5.1 or later and dune 3.20 or later.
- Melange 7 or later, for the code that runs in the browser.
- mlx, the JSX syntax for OCaml: the opam packages [mlx] (the commands
  [mlx-pp] and [ocamlmerlin-mlx]) and [ocamlformat-mlx].
- [jsonkit] and [jsonkit-melange], when the app derives its codecs (see
  {!page-recipes.json}).
- Node.js, for esbuild and Tailwind in the client build.

Silica is not on opam yet. Pin a checkout of the repository with
[opam pin add silica <path>] (and the same for [silica-ui]), or put the
checkout inside your dune workspace.

{1:project The dune project}

The project file declares the mlx dialect, so that dune reads [.mlx] files as
OCaml with JSX:

From [dune-project]:
{@dune skip[
(dialect
 (name mlx)
 (implementation
  (extension mlx)
  (merlin_reader mlx)
  (format
   (run ocamlformat-mlx %{input-file}))
  (preprocess
   (run mlx-pp %{input-file}))))
]}

It also needs [(using melange 1.0)] at the top.

Code that the server and the browser share goes in one folder with no stanza.
Two libraries copy it: one for native OCaml and one for Melange. The examples
of this documentation use this setup. The native library:

From [docs/examples/native/dune]:
{@dune skip[
(copy_files ../shared/*.{ml,mlx})

(library
 (name silica_docs_examples_native)
 (wrapped false)
 (modes byte native)
 (libraries silica silica-ui)
 (preprocess
  (pps silica.ppx)))
]}

The Melange library is the same with [(modes melange)] and the preprocessors
[silica.ppx melange.ppx]. The ppx [silica.ppx] turns the JSX elements into
calls of {!Silica.Node.element} (see {!page-components.jsx}). An app that
derives JSON codecs needs a different library for each side; see
{!page-recipes.json}.

The browser entry is a [melange.emit] stanza over the Melange library and
[silica.browser]:

{@dune file=examples/client/dune[
(melange.emit
 (target out)
 (alias docs-examples-js)
 (modules tutorial_client)
 (module_systems
  (esm mjs))
 (libraries silica_docs_examples_melange silica.browser silica.json-melange))
]}

A bundler such as esbuild makes the file for the page from the output of this
stanza. See [example/client/dune] in the repository for the esbuild rule, and
{!page-recipes.hosting} for the server.

{1:first A first component}

A component is a machine and a view. This lamp has two states, [Off] and
[On], and one event, [Toggle]. The view shows a text and a button for each
state.

{@mlx file=examples/shared/doc_lamp.mlx[
open Silica

type event = Toggle

module States = struct
  type _ t = Off : unit t | On : unit t
end

module M = Machine.Make (States)

let machine =
  M.make ~id:"lamp" ~init:(M.at Off ())
    [
      M.state Off "off" ~to_:[ On ] (fun () Toggle -> M.goto On ());
      M.state On "on" ~to_:[ Off ] (fun () Toggle -> M.goto Off ());
    ]

let view (snapshot : M.snapshot) : event Node.t =
  match snapshot with
  | M.At (Off, ()) ->
      <div>
        <p>"The lamp is off."</p> <button on_press=Toggle>"Turn on"</button>
      </div>
  | M.At (On, ()) ->
      <div>
        <p>"The lamp is on."</p> <button on_press=Toggle>"Turn off"</button>
      </div>

let component = Component.make machine view
]}

The same code runs on native OCaml. A test can step the machine and print the
HTML of the view:

{@ocaml[
# let lamp = Doc_lamp.machine;;
val lamp : (Doc_lamp.M.snapshot, Doc_lamp.event) Silica.Machine.t = <abstr>
# let snapshot, _ = Silica.Machine.start lamp;;
val snapshot : Doc_lamp.M.snapshot =
  Doc_lamp.M.At (Doc_lamp.States.Off, <poly>)
# let snapshot, _, _ = Silica.Machine.send lamp snapshot Doc_lamp.Toggle;;
val snapshot : Doc_lamp.M.snapshot =
  Doc_lamp.M.At (Doc_lamp.States.On, <poly>)
# Silica.Render.to_string (Doc_lamp.view snapshot);;
- : string = "<div><p>The lamp is on.</p><button>Turn off</button></div>"
]}

In the browser, one call of {!Silica_browser.mount} paints it into an element
of the page. The {{!page-tutorial.browser}tutorial} shows the client file.

